# SwiftUI

Use these production invariants to build high-frame-rate, state-safe **SwiftUI** applications that respect the **AttributeGraph** dependency engine, modern **Observation (`@Observable`)** data flow, and the **Liquid Glass (iOS 26 / macOS 26 Tahoe+)** optical design system.

---

## 1. AttributeGraph, View Identity & Diffing Invariants

SwiftUI's runtime maps view hierarchies into a directed acyclic **AttributeGraph**. Every `View` struct is a lightweight value recipe; SwiftUI preserves `@State`, focus, and transitions strictly through **Structural Identity** (type + branch position) and **Explicit Identity** (`Identifiable` / `.id(_:)`).

- **DO** extract distinct subtrees into separate `View` structs rather than splitting a large `body` into `@ViewBuilder` computed properties or helper methods on the same struct. Separate `View` structs create independent nodes in `AttributeGraph`, allowing SwiftUI to short-circuit child `body` evaluation when child inputs are unchanged.
- **DO** keep leaf `View` structs **POD-friendly (Plain Old Data)** where possible (value properties only, no stored closures) so SwiftUI can compare old and new view structs via fast `memcmp` field inspection. If a hot-path row view requires closures or complex inputs, conform the view to `Equatable` (comparing only rendering-relevant data IDs/values) and apply `.equatable()`.
- **DO** preserve **Structural Identity** by keeping view types stable across state changes. Prefer ternary expressions inside modifiers (`.opacity(isVisible ? 1 : 0)`) over top-level `if / else` branches when toggling visual properties of the same component.
- **DO** use stable, domain-backed `Identifiable` IDs in `ForEach(items)` (`Item.ID`).
- **DON'T** use `AnyView` (erases static type trees and forces runtime graph teardown), `ForEach(items.indices, id: \.self)` on mutable collections (breaks insertions/deletions and corrupts row state), or inline `UUID()` generation inside `body` / computed properties.
- **DON'T** attach `.id(UUID())` or changing `.id(value)` to scroll containers or heavy subtrees as a "force refresh" hack—changing `.id(_:)` destroys the entire view subtree, cancels active `.task` lifecycles, and resets scroll/focus state.

```swift
// GOOD: Isolated AttributeGraph node + POD-friendly value inputs
struct MetricRowView: View, Equatable {
    let title: String
    let formattedValue: String
    let isSelected: Bool
    let onSelect: () -> Void

    static func == (lhs: Self, rhs: Self) -> Bool {
        lhs.title == rhs.title &&
        lhs.formattedValue == rhs.formattedValue &&
        lhs.isSelected == rhs.isSelected
    }

    var body: some View {
        Button(action: onSelect) {
            HStack {
                Text(title)
                Spacer()
                Text(formattedValue)
                    .foregroundStyle(.secondary)
                    .contentTransition(.numericText())
            }
        }
    }
}
```

