## 2. Fine-Grained Observation (`@Observable`) & State Ownership

Modern SwiftUI uses the **Observation framework (`@Observable`)**, which registers per-property read tracking inside `AttributeGraph` only for the specific stored properties accessed during a view's `body` evaluation.

### Property Wrapper Selection Matrix

| Scenario | Required Declaration | Why (Engine Behavior) |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| View-local value state (`Bool`, `String`, struct) | `@State private var isExpanded = false` | Allocates persistent view-scoped storage tied to view identity. Always mark `private`. |
| View creates & owns an `@Observable` reference model | `@State private var model = EditorModel()` | Preserves the reference instance across parent `body` re-evaluations (replaces `@StateObject`). |
| View reads an `@Observable` model passed from parent | `let model: EditorModel` | Zero wrapper overhead; `AttributeGraph` tracks only the properties read inside `body`. |
| View needs two-way `$model.prop` bindings to an `@Observable` | `@Bindable var model: EditorModel` | Synthesizes `Binding<Value>` projections from an `@Observable` reference (can also be declared inline inside `body`: `@Bindable var model = model`). |
| Shared dependency injected via Environment | `@Environment(SessionStore.self) private var session` | Reads an `@Observable` object injected via `.environment(sessionStore)`. |
| Custom `EnvironmentValues` / `FocusedValues` / `ContainerValues` | `@Entry var accentTint: Color = .blue` | Macro that generates `EnvironmentKey` and getter/setter boilerplate automatically. |

- **DO** annotate UI-bound `@Observable` classes with `@MainActor` (unless the target uses Swift 6.2+ default `@MainActor` module isolation) so mutations never hop threads mid-transaction.
- **DO** mark non-UI caches, tasks, or formatters inside an `@Observable` class with `@ObservationIgnored` so internal bookkeeping does not register false `AttributeGraph` dependencies.
- **DON'T** use legacy `ObservableObject`, `@Published`, `@StateObject`, `@ObservedObject`, or `@EnvironmentObject` in modern SwiftUI code.
- **DON'T** instantiate an `@Observable` object directly in a view's property declaration without `@State` (`var model = MyModel()`), or inside `init` by assigning directly to `@State`—doing so re-allocates a fresh object or gets ignored on every parent redraw.
- **DON'T** pipe high-frequency continuous events (60–120Hz scroll offsets, drag translations, audio meters) into broad `@Observable` model properties read by root views; scope continuous state to leaf views with `@GestureState` / local `@State` or render-thread `.visualEffect`.

```swift
import Observation
import SwiftUI

extension EnvironmentValues {
    @Entry var densityScale: Double = 1.0
}

@MainActor
@Observable
final class DocumentStore {
    var title: String = "Untitled"
    var wordCount: Int = 0
    @ObservationIgnored private var autosaveTask: Task<Void, Never>?
}

struct DocumentHeaderView: View {
    @Bindable var store: DocumentStore
    @Environment(\.densityScale) private var densityScale

    var body: some View {
        VStack(alignment: .leading, spacing: 8 * densityScale) {
            TextField("Document Title", text: $store.title)
                .font(.title2.bold())
            Text("\(store.wordCount) words")
                .font(.caption)
                .foregroundStyle(.secondary)
        }
    }
}
```

