## 2. Scene, Window & Liquid Glass Hierarchy (macOS 15 Sequoia / macOS 26 Tahoe)

### A. Multi-Window & Single-Instance Scene Discipline
- **DO** choose the exact `Scene` type for the window's role:
  - `WindowGroup`: Multi-window document or workspace viewers (`Cmd+N` spawns a new instance).
  - `Window("Title", id: "id")`: Single-instance windows (About box, Activity viewer, Connection manager). Calling `openWindow(id:)` brings the existing window to the front instead of spawning duplicates.
  - `UtilityWindow("Inspector", id: "inspector")`: Floating tool palettes that hide automatically when the app resigns active status.
  - `Settings`: App preferences bound to `Cmd+,`.
- **DO** pair `.windowResizability(.contentSize)` with explicit `.frame(minWidth:maxWidth:minHeight:maxHeight:)` on the root view when constraining window bounds.
- **DON'T** hardcode fixed `.frame(width: 800, height: 600)` on a `WindowGroup` root view without `.windowResizability(...)`—users will still be able to resize the outer `NSWindow`, leaving dead gray padding around your view.

### B. Liquid Glass & Visual Material Hierarchy (macOS 26 Tahoe)
- **DO** restrict Liquid Glass (`.glassEffect()`) and translucent vibrancy (`NSVisualEffectView`) strictly to the **navigation and chrome layer**: sidebars, toolbars, floating HUD overlays, and popovers floating above scrolled content.
- **DO** keep sidebar (`NavigationSplitView` source list) icons monochrome (`primary`—black in Light Mode, white in Dark Mode) on macOS Tahoe so they remain legible over dynamically refracted background content.
- **DON'T** apply `.glassEffect()` to scrollable content areas, text editors, data tables, or stacked glass-on-glass containers.

